{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Motion of Light in Water: Sex and Science Fiction Writing in the East Village is an autobiography by science fiction author Samuel R. Delany in which he recounts his experiences as growing up a gay African American, as well as some of his time in an interracial and open marriage with Marilyn Hacker.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It describes encounters with Albert Einstein, Bob Dylan, and Stokely Carmichael, a dinner with W. H. Auden, and a phone call to James Baldwin.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Hazel Carby called it one of two contemporary autobiographies that are "absolutely central to any consideration of black manhood" (the other being that of Miles Davis).","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Among many cultural events of the decade that he witnessed, Delany recounts his attendance at the first New York City performance of artist Allan Kaprow's "18 Happenings in 6 Parts", the 1959 performance piece that, for many, marks the end of modernism and the beginning of postmodernism.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In section 17.4 of the University of Minnesota Press edition, he describes the event and its venue, and speculates on its artistic significance.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The introduction puts an emphasis on the idea of the unreliable narrator; Delany's accounts often contrast his life as it "felt" to ways in which it actually occurred.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" In the chapter, The Future Is in the Present of the book "Cruising Utopia" by José Esteban Munoz, Delany's The Motion of Light in the Water serves to explain how the future, as a formed of utopia, can be "glimpsed" in the present through what Delany employed as "the massed bodies" of sexual dissidence.","title":"The Motion of Light in Water"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Spellsinger is a series of fantasy novels written by Alan Dean Foster.","title":"Spellsinger"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" At present the series consists of eight books and, although there was a significant gap between the writing of book six and book seven, it seems unlikely that any more will be written.","title":"Spellsinger"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The AAnn are a fictional species of reptilians in Alan Dean Foster's Humanx Commonwealth series.","title":"List of Humanx Commonwealth races"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They are described as lizard-like carnivores with flexibility and speed superior to that of most men, although they are generally shorter and somewhat weaker physically.","title":"List of Humanx Commonwealth races"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They are covered in small scales and prefer hot, arid worlds with plenty of sand.","title":"List of Humanx Commonwealth races"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Phylogenesis (1999) is a science fiction novel by American writer Alan Dean Foster.","title":"Phylogenesis (novel)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the first novel in Foster's "Founding of the Commonwealth Trilogy".","title":"Phylogenesis (novel)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Glory Lane (1987) is a science fiction novel written by Alan Dean Foster.","title":"Glory Lane"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The book takes place outside of either of Foster’s two usual universes, Spellsinger and the Humanx Commonwealth.","title":"Glory Lane"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Deluge Drivers (1987) is a science fiction novel by American writer Alan Dean Foster.","title":"The Deluge Drivers"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the final entry in Foster's "Icerigger Trilogy" of books taking place in the Humanx Commonwealth book series.","title":"The Deluge Drivers"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The two earlier books in the series are "Icerigger" and "Mission to Moulokin."","title":"The Deluge Drivers"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Samuel R. Delany
